AI Isn’t Making Us More Productive—It’s Making More Noise
The Productivity Illusion of AI
We Don’t Need More Output. We Need Better Signal
AI and the Rising Cost of Noise
Core Thesis

AI is dramatically increasing output, but not necessarily value—creating a growing “noise problem” that overwhelms individuals and organizations. The real opportunity isn’t more productivity, but better filtering, prioritization, and discernment.

Outline
1. Hook: The Productivity Paradox
Open with the CEO anecdote (concise, vivid)
Describe the cartoon:
Person uses AI to expand a bullet into a paragraph
Recipient uses AI to compress it back into a bullet
Punchline:
“We’re not creating value—we’re inflating and compressing the same information.”

Point: AI may be accelerating a loop of performative productivity.

2. The Illusion of Increased Output
Acknowledge the truth:
Yes, AI increases throughput
Yes, individuals can produce more content faster
But introduce the tension:
More output ≠ more clarity, insight, or value
Reframe:
Productivity at the individual level can create overload at the system level

Key line idea:
“What feels like productivity for one person often becomes burden for everyone else.”

3. When Productivity Becomes Noise
Expand on the systemic effect:
Emails, documents, posts, summaries—all multiplying
Everyone now has to process more
Introduce concept:
Noise inflation — the rising volume of low-signal information
Consequence:
Attention becomes the real bottleneck
4. Anecdote: Scientists and AI Adoption
Shift to your personal experience:
AI tools marketed as productivity enhancers for scientists
Reality:
Many scientists aren’t using them
Explore possible reasons:
Overloaded already (no bandwidth to adopt new tools)
Workflow friction
Skepticism or unclear ROI
Insight:
The problem isn’t just tool availability—it’s cognitive capacity

Point: You can’t “optimize” people who are already maxed out.

5. The Real Problem: Signal vs. Noise
Introduce your core argument clearly:
The bottleneck is no longer creation—it’s discernment
Use analogy:
Separating signal from noise / wheat from chaff
Reframe AI’s role:
Not “help us create more”
But “help us identify what matters”
6. A Zero-Sum Future of Content?
Present the risk:
If everyone produces more, no one benefits proportionally
Describe the loop:
More AI → more content → more filtering → more tools → more content
Call it out:
“We risk building an economy of synthetic busyness.”
7. Broader Implications (Politics, Media, Society)
Connect to larger trends:
Misinformation
AI-generated content
Social media overload
Effects:
Difficulty distinguishing truth
People disengaging (“I don’t believe anything”)
Or retreating into confirmation bias

Tone: Slightly sobering, not alarmist.

8. What We Should Be Building Instead
Shift to constructive angle:
Tools that:
Filter, not flood
Prioritize, not expand
Clarify, not decorate
Human skill emphasis:
Judgment
Taste
Discernment
Organizational shift:
Reward clarity over volume
Reduce unnecessary communication
9. Closing: A Different Definition of Productivity
Reframe productivity:
Not “how much we produce”
But “how much truly matters”
End with a strong line:

Examples:

“If AI helps us say more, but understand less, it’s not progress.”
“The future doesn’t need more content. It needs more clarity.”
“The real advantage won’t go to those who produce the most—but to those who filter best.”
